Z||000011||theogre||09-26-2001||08:47 PM||||Well.... Metri-Pack, shown above, and Weather Pack are available thru many sources. Pep Boys and many others carry them thru the MotorMite product lines. Anyone that carries MotorMite can likely order it if they don't already have it in stock. You can get tools, loose parts and premade sets from them. For comparison... Their price for a MAP sensor with pigtails is around $15.<P>All Weather Pack, including MAP sensor and old style IAC/TPS, can be "shelled" without cutting the wires. All you need is a good shell and the terminal release tool from MotorMite or Lisle. (Instructions and links for this are in the wire article too.) It takes less than 10 minutes to reshell a Weather Pack connector. The MAP sensor shell is easy to find used but the old IAC and TPS are getting hard to come by in good used.<P>Metri-Pack "Pull to Seat" connectors, used for new style TPS/MAT/ECT and others, cannot be replaced without cutting. You either need to replace the terminal or splice on a new preassembled set. They are more of a pain in the ass then dificult.<P>To make "Pull to Seat" ends you first thread the wire thru the seal and shell like a bead. Then you strip the end and crimp on a terminal with pliers made for "open barrel" terminals. Once the terminal is crimped you pull the wire back thru the shell untill it stops. The hardest part is getting the wire accuratly crimped. If the crimp is weak the wire will come out of the terminal fairly easily.<P>Links to a several sources of connectors and tools are in the wire service article in my cave. I haven't dealt with all of those companies. Most are rather well established companies. I think White Racing is just some guy doing stuff on the side or a small firm.<P>One good source of good used shells... Salvage yards that handle allot of insurance wrecks. These yards handle more new cars than most yards. I've also found the connectors from even old FWD cars are often in better shape than ours simply because they have cooler engine bays. I've got good shells for nearly all my connectors. Now I've just got to get time to fool with them.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||09-26-2001||10:03 PM||||Napa Auto Parts also has many of these connectors on their racks, and they can order the ones they don't have. They are only a few dollars a piece and they also have the pins ans seals. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Boomtastic||09-27-2001||07:57 PM||||Yes, I understand that there are other sources that sell these connectors, however most people would rather point and click on a site and have them shipped to their doorstep than take time out of their busy life and run to the store to get them. After all, they've been driving with broken clips for eons now - what's another couple of days? The question was not "Where can you buy these?", it was "Which style would you buy if given a choice?"||||reg||4
